WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:05.590 --> 00:00:12.020
May we then move on to item nine, which is
Genomics. I am going to invite Tim Kelsey

00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:17.990
to introduce the theme and Bruce Keogh to
follow. Tim, over to you.

00:00:17.990 --> 00:00:24.990
Tim Kelsey: The clinical benefit opportunities
that genomic science presents to all health

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:32.350
systems in the world, but particularly to
the NHS, is enormous. I think Bruce will say

00:00:33.290 --> 00:00:40.290
something that in a second. I think we are,
very much, at an historic moment for our patients.

00:00:40.570 --> 00:00:46.690
This particular paper is very important for
NHS England. Subject to the Board's approval,

00:00:46.690 --> 00:00:53.690
it launches a programme to invite trusts around
the country to participate in the first major

00:00:55.649 --> 00:01:01.110
pilot project to investigate the potential
of this new science for patients. As the Board

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:06.580
will know, the Prime Minister challenged the
NHS as a whole to sequence 100,000 genomes

00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:13.580
by 2017. That is the first step, really, in
unlocking the potential value of this for

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:15.759
patients.

00:01:15.759 --> 00:01:22.140
Today, subject to the Board's approval, we
will launch a programme to encourage trusts

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:29.140
to partner with us in the delivery of samples
from patients who consent to that process.

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:34.450
By October, we will be able to announce the
trusts we want to partner with. We will therefore

00:01:34.450 --> 00:01:41.450
want to come back to give you an update at
that point. In this programme we are working

00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:47.110
very closely with the Department of Health.
They have the lead responsibility for it.

00:01:47.110 --> 00:01:54.110
However, we are also working with Genomics
England. That is the body appointed to work

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:59.390
on delivering the sequences and the associated
data with us.

00:01:59.390 --> 00:02:04.220
I think that working relationship is excellent.
It has been very good. Obviously, Malcolm,

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:11.220
you are on the Board of Genomics England itself
as our representative. We will be working

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:18.260
closely with them on developing the ITT formally
when we start to partner with trusts.

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:23.129
I just wanted to report to the Board that
we spent a lot of time working on the governance

00:02:23.129 --> 00:02:26.750
around this programme. It is clearly very
important at one level that the public and

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:30.870
patients are taken with us on this journey.
It is important this is something that is

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:34.750
really careful and considered about the way
in which it represents its benefits to people.

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:41.750
As I say, it is very much a consent based
initiative. However, alongside that the Department

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:47.650
of Health hosts an assurance Board. That makes
sure the programme runs according to its objective.

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:54.650
However, it also does take public interest
very carefully. We inside NHS England have

00:02:54.959 --> 00:03:01.560
our own assurance Board, which I chair. Additionally,
Bruce is shortly to start chairing the genomics

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:06.189
clinical advisory group. That will help us,
as NHS England, to determine the shape of

00:03:06.189 --> 00:03:08.129
the programme as we develop it.

00:03:08.129 --> 00:03:13.680
Perhaps I might leave it and finally just
note that the Board is asked to consider three

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:16.709
things. First, we should note the benefits
of this potential programme. Secondly, we

00:03:16.709 --> 00:03:22.079
should agree the timetable for the selection
of clinical partners. Those partners, once

00:03:22.079 --> 00:03:28.109
selected, will be denominated as NHS Genomics
Medicine Centres, for the record. Thirdly,

00:03:28.109 --> 00:03:33.299
we should ideally approve the draft prospectus.
That will be the basis of that site selection

00:03:33.299 --> 00:03:35.650
and it is before you as well.

00:03:35.650 --> 00:03:35.969
Bruce, I do not know whether you wanted to
add anything.

00:03:35.969 --> 00:03:36.150
Malcolm Grant: Bruce, over to you.

00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:43.150
Bruce Keogh: Thank you very much, Chair. It
would be easy to think this is just another

00:03:45.290 --> 00:03:51.389
kind of slightly obscure, or technocratic,
initiative. However, it is something that

00:03:51.389 --> 00:03:55.819
is really big. It is going to be big for everyone.
It is going to be big for the citizens of

00:03:55.819 --> 00:03:59.349
this country. It is going to be big for patients.
It is going to be big for the NHS and it is

00:03:59.349 --> 00:04:03.650
going to be big for our society at large.

00:04:03.650 --> 00:04:10.650
In the history of medicine, a handful of things
happen at moments in time which take us to

00:04:11.639 --> 00:04:18.639
places that we do not expect, do not understand
and bring enormous benefit. A typical example

00:04:19.049 --> 00:04:25.060
would be the invention of the microscope in
the United Kingdom. That might have seemed

00:04:25.060 --> 00:04:28.470
a bit obscure at the time. It was something
just of interest to a handful of biologist.

00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:34.870
However, it eventually led to the transformation
of our understanding of nature, pathology

00:04:34.870 --> 00:04:41.870
of disease and so on and so forth. In particular,
it led to the discovery of the cell.

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:48.610
One of the other big things would have been
the advent, or the discovery, of X rays. X

00:04:48.610 --> 00:04:55.610
rays suddenly enabled people to look inside
the body. This, subsequently, was to transform

00:04:56.500 --> 00:05:03.500
the practice of medicine and many aspects
of science. However, it was met in differing

00:05:04.750 --> 00:05:11.590
measures of enthusiasm and fear by the public
at the time, as indeed, possibly, a genomics

00:05:11.590 --> 00:05:14.710
programme might be.

00:05:14.710 --> 00:05:21.710
Another major advance from the United Kingdom
was the discovery of DNA, how it joined together

00:05:21.830 --> 00:05:26.550
and how it worked. It enabled us to understand
the concept of the gene. The gene is a kind

00:05:26.550 --> 00:05:33.550
of subcellular structure which helps to define
the nature of an organism, how people work

00:05:39.530 --> 00:05:44.669
and of how things happen in the body.

00:05:44.669 --> 00:05:50.580
When you put all of those genes together and
you start to try and understand them, that

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:57.580
gives rise to the science of genomics. The
first sequencing of the whole human genome,

00:05:58.849 --> 00:06:05.849
rather than just sequencing a single gene,
took 13 years and cost $2 billion to sequence.

00:06:07.979 --> 00:06:14.979
The turning point we have reached now is that
we can sequence a human genome now in somewhere

00:06:17.080 --> 00:06:22.539
around one, or maybe two or three, hours.
Furthermore, we can do it at a fraction of

00:06:22.539 --> 00:06:29.539
that cost. Estimates vary, however it is certainly
under $1000 and probably closer to $200 to

00:06:29.780 --> 00:06:30.250
$300.

00:06:30.250 --> 00:06:35.789
We are seeing the dawn of a new technology.
It has suddenly shifted from highly complex

00:06:35.789 --> 00:06:41.810
laboratories to availability for use in common
clinical practice.

00:06:41.810 --> 00:06:47.770
We already know, from studying single genes,
that it has enabled to understand diseases

00:06:47.770 --> 00:06:53.509
such as cancer. It has enabled us to understand
what mutations, by which I mean faulty genes,

00:06:53.509 --> 00:07:00.060
can bring to disease. Most importantly, it
can enable us to focus very specific types

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:03.120
of treatment on different diseases.

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:09.520
When I was a student, or even when I was training
in surgery initially, there were about four

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:15.659
different types of breast cancer and a limited
number of treatments. Now we know, from looking

00:07:15.659 --> 00:07:20.389
at genetic sequences, that there are over
40 types. There are probably many more than

00:07:20.389 --> 00:07:26.780
that. However, we are able to target treatments
very specifically. That targeting is based

00:07:26.780 --> 00:07:33.780
on an understanding of the genetic sequence
that underpins that cancer. Adenocarcinoma

00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:39.000
of the lung is similar. The treatment of that
is almost entirely defined by the genetic

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:41.439
sequence that underpins that particular cancer.

00:07:41.439 --> 00:07:48.439
Those developments are just the result of
understanding tiny bits of the gene. Once

00:07:48.870 --> 00:07:55.159
we start to sequence the whole of the human
genome, that gets us into completely new territory.

00:07:55.159 --> 00:08:02.159
It will enable us to understand much more
about the genetics that underpin disease.

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:10.120
It will enable us to predict the likelihood
of different individuals ultimately developing

00:08:12.909 --> 00:08:19.759
certain disease processes. That may be cancer,
cardiovascular disease or others. Being able

00:08:19.759 --> 00:08:26.759
to predict that will enable us to put into
place preventative measures. Those measures

00:08:26.789 --> 00:08:33.789
may relate to screening 
or they may relate to dietary behaviour, for
example.

00:08:38.480 --> 00:08:43.830
The exciting here is that most of the technologies
that enable us to do this were developed in

00:08:43.830 --> 00:08:49.260
the United Kingdom. It therefore kind of seems
right and natural that we should use those

00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:56.260
technologies that are developed here to develop
our own science and bring it directly to bear

00:08:57.860 --> 00:09:03.700
on the health our own citizens. This is, to
my mind, a massive opportunity for our NHS

00:09:03.700 --> 00:09:09.280
and it is a massive opportunity for our scientists
in this country to help lead the world.

00:09:09.280 --> 00:09:16.280
In the paper we are proposing that we will
sequence 100,000 human genomes. It will be

00:09:18.420 --> 00:09:25.420
done from consenting volunteers. That is based
on the premise that this country, I think,

00:09:27.960 --> 00:09:34.710
has got a very long history of medical science
based on social philanthropy. In other words:

00:09:34.710 --> 00:09:41.710
people who are willing to subject themselves
to consented procedures which lead to the

00:09:44.850 --> 00:09:49.530
benefit of the next generation and to the
benefit of their fellow citizens. However,

00:09:49.530 --> 00:09:56.530
I think it is going to raise some pretty complex
scientific and societal issues. All of those

00:09:56.780 --> 00:10:01.170
will simply be driven by the science.

00:10:01.170 --> 00:10:08.170
Different societies are going to find it more
difficult to deal with this than others. We

00:10:10.940 --> 00:10:16.210
hear a debate about our NHS reaching its sell
by date, that somehow or other we are not

00:10:16.210 --> 00:10:19.890
fit for the future. I find myself thinking,
in particular, that I would invite people

00:10:19.890 --> 00:10:26.080
to reflect on what the impact of this kind
of science, where you can predict disease,

00:10:26.080 --> 00:10:31.670
will have on insurance based, or privately
funded healthcare systems. You can predict

00:10:31.670 --> 00:10:35.580
that somebody might suffer a whole series
of different diseases. Think about what that

00:10:35.580 --> 00:10:42.580
means for equality of access to services.
Our NHS is based on a tax funded, risk pooled

00:10:48.880 --> 00:10:55.220
system, where the whole population shares
the risk for anybody that is ill. I therefore

00:10:55.220 --> 00:11:01.900
put it to you that our NHS is not at its sell
by date. It is getting to its just ready date.

00:11:01.900 --> 00:11:08.350
It is something we need to really consider
in the debate that is to follow.

00:11:08.350 --> 00:11:12.310
I put it to you that the time has come for
the science of genomics, which has had a few

00:11:12.310 --> 00:11:19.310
false dawns. It is going to be really good
for patients. It is going to be really exciting

00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:28.000
for those who deliver the service: clinicians.
I think we have the potential to be here really

00:11:29.820 --> 00:11:36.440
at the dawn of this science and to develop
it. It is going to offer fantastic opportunities

00:11:36.440 --> 00:11:43.440
for UK PLC, in terms of generating business.

00:11:44.720 --> 00:11:51.460
Tim Kelsey: Can I just add to that excellent,
inspiring overview? Operationally this is

00:11:51.460 --> 00:11:56.170
going to be very difficult. We should not
underestimate the risks. There are risks in

00:11:56.170 --> 00:12:00.450
sample collection itself. Even the science
around how you collect samples at this volume

00:12:00.450 --> 00:12:07.450
and successfully extract DNA is largely not
known well. Furthermore, the method for collecting

00:12:07.910 --> 00:12:13.100
data about the individuals in order that the
genetic sequence can be made sense of is also

00:12:13.100 --> 00:12:16.520
very much at the cutting edge. We therefore
have a lot to learn. However, those are clearly

00:12:16.520 --> 00:12:18.040
risks that clearly, as Bruce describes, are
well worth taking.

00:12:18.040 --> 00:12:23.330
Malcolm Grant: I just have a couple of observations
on that, if I may. First of all I would like

00:12:23.330 --> 00:12:28.820
to stress that Genomics England Limited, of
which I am a Board member, is wholly owned

00:12:28.820 --> 00:12:35.080
by the Department of Health. This is, therefore,
a vehicle for a joint venture between us and

00:12:35.080 --> 00:12:40.490
the Department of Health, with strong support
from the National Institute of Health Research.

00:12:40.490 --> 00:12:45.790
It also includes engagement from the Wellcome
Trust and others. They are excited, I think,

00:12:45.790 --> 00:12:51.270
to advance this as a major scientific opportunity
for England.

00:12:51.270 --> 00:12:56.010
I will now turn to the second issue. I think
it is now impossible to go to an international

00:12:56.010 --> 00:13:02.060
conference on any major disease condition
without hearing a succession of papers around

00:13:02.060 --> 00:13:07.400
the genetic construct of that condition. Those
papers will aim to understand the identification

00:13:07.400 --> 00:13:14.400
of different mutations and their association
with different types of disease. I think the

00:13:14.810 --> 00:13:20.600
difference between that and what is being
proposed here is twofold.

00:13:20.600 --> 00:13:26.560
One difference is the ability to sequence
the entire genome of a human being. This is

00:13:26.560 --> 00:13:30.860
as opposed to genetics, which concentrates
on one or two genes. The second difference

00:13:30.860 --> 00:13:37.860
is the ability to do this at a population
level. It may be able, potentially, to discover

00:13:38.230 --> 00:13:43.210
new genetic correlations which are, at the
moment, beyond our comprehension or ability

00:13:43.210 --> 00:13:45.200
to discover.

00:13:45.200 --> 00:13:52.200
I regard the 100,000 Genomes Project as, effectively,
a pilot. It is, as Tim rightly says, accompanied

00:13:53.060 --> 00:13:59.310
by a degree of risk. However, a lot of this
is a degree of risk about things that are

00:13:59.310 --> 00:14:06.310
unknown. For example, how can we safely and
satisfactorily acquire reliable samples which

00:14:07.800 --> 00:14:12.590
can then be sequenced? There are also questions
about how the samples are stored and for how

00:14:12.590 --> 00:14:18.540
long and under what conditions. There are
quite significant questions about the way

00:14:18.540 --> 00:14:24.520
in which the sequencing takes place and how
the data are stored and processed. Furthermore,

00:14:24.520 --> 00:14:30.260
as Tim says, there are questions as to how
the phenotypic data are then mapped against

00:14:30.260 --> 00:14:37.030
the genotypic data so as to give us the insights
as to how, on a population basis, we can map

00:14:37.030 --> 00:14:43.470
genetic mutations with other features of the
population.

00:14:43.470 --> 00:14:49.030
All of this is being based on individual patient
informed consent. There is a lot of argument

00:14:49.030 --> 00:14:55.350
on the Board of Genomics England about what
that consent looks like, that it is not limited

00:14:55.350 --> 00:15:01.250
in time to a single transaction but it has
a continuing set of ethical responsibilities

00:15:01.250 --> 00:15:07.830
and relations to those who are part of the
programme. The 100,000 genomes does not signify

00:15:07.830 --> 00:15:14.720
100,000 patients. For example, in relation
to rare genetic diseases it is often necessary

00:15:14.720 --> 00:15:20.450
to sequence not only the patient, but perhaps
also the tumour and close familial relatives

00:15:20.450 --> 00:15:23.980
of the patient. That aims to try to understand
how this looks in different generations. Indeed,

00:15:23.980 --> 00:15:26.830
it also aims to understand how it looks in
the same generation.

00:15:26.830 --> 00:15:33.830
I think the advantages of doing this in the
UK are really quite profound. The first is

00:15:34.240 --> 00:15:39.730
the scientific leadership that I think the
UK has already evinced by being, with the

00:15:39.730 --> 00:15:45.510
US, the partner is the first sequencing of
the human genome back in 2002. The second

00:15:45.510 --> 00:15:51.010
is the strong interest of our universities
in participating in this programme and understanding

00:15:51.010 --> 00:15:57.580
the research potential for the population
at large. The third is our, I would say, unique

00:15:57.580 --> 00:16:01.900
situation of having a comprehensive health
service in which this can be undertaken as

00:16:01.900 --> 00:16:07.720
a joint venture between two public services.
It is a joint venture between a comprehensive

00:16:07.720 --> 00:16:14.720
health service and also our universities and
their public funding environment.

00:16:17.340 --> 00:16:24.340
We see so many challenges to the sustainability
of a fragile health service in current economic

00:16:26.090 --> 00:16:29.250
conditions. Therefore, I think it is exciting
at this time to understand there is technology

00:16:29.250 --> 00:16:36.150
that is capable of being transformative in
the way in which we identify conditions. We

00:16:36.150 --> 00:16:41.060
do diagnosis, we can do prevention. We can
better understand prognosis and we can better

00:16:41.060 --> 00:16:46.400
understand treatment with pharmacogenetics
is more closely attuned to the genetic profile

00:16:46.400 --> 00:16:47.540
of individual.

00:16:47.540 --> 00:16:54.540
I think it would be wrong to overstate the
promise because this is early stages. However,

00:16:54.990 --> 00:17:01.180
I think we can assure the Board that there
is an advantage for England and the UK more

00:17:01.180 --> 00:17:07.640
generally over other countries who are, I
think, looking at this with great interest.

00:17:07.640 --> 00:17:14.549
Let me invite comments from the Board. I will
then ask you to give particular attention

00:17:14.549 --> 00:17:21.019
to the questions that are being put to you.
Margaret?

00:17:21.019 --> 00:17:25.199
Margaret Casely Hayford: This is so exciting.
Really, really, really exciting. Thank you

00:17:25.199 --> 00:17:27.379
very much for your explanation, which took
me through something very complex in a way

00:17:27.379 --> 00:17:33.000
that I could get to grips with it. That was
very helpful, thank you Bruce.

00:17:33.000 --> 00:17:40.000
I have one question for Tim and one, I suppose,
sort of suggestion. The question for Tim is

00:17:42.700 --> 00:17:49.700
in connection with the process. Can you just
give me comfort about the level to which we

00:17:51.620 --> 00:17:58.620
are being supported by legal advice on state
aid[?]. I just want to be sure that we have

00:18:03.059 --> 00:18:10.059
asked ourselves the right questions and come
out at the right outcome. It is such a cutting

00:18:11.919 --> 00:18:16.759
edge and really exciting prospect. Therefore,
I can imagine that providers, internationally

00:18:16.759 --> 00:18:23.759
as well as nationally, that might not be successful
might actually want to look closely at the

00:18:26.970 --> 00:18:31.990
process. There is a prospect and we obviously
want to make sure we are robust.

00:18:31.990 --> 00:18:38.990
The second point is, really, I think because
it is opening such an important gateway it

00:18:39.669 --> 00:18:46.669
would probably be advisable for us to build
into that support for the consenters. That

00:18:48.549 --> 00:18:55.549
support would be a body of professional protection
and guidance as they go through the process.

00:19:01.210 --> 00:19:07.850
It is going to be something quite revolutionary
for them personally and their families.

00:19:07.850 --> 00:19:14.539
Tim Kelsey: Just so that the Board is clear,
the responsibility NHS England has it to the

00:19:14.539 --> 00:19:21.129
collection of samples to power the genomic
sequencing and associated data. Therefore,

00:19:21.129 --> 00:19:28.129
we will be running an open, competitive process
for trusts to apply to be part of the programme.

00:19:29.379 --> 00:19:29.870
We have taken legal advice to ensure we are
running that process. Trusts will receive

00:19:29.870 --> 00:19:35.080
certain financial incentives to do that. However,
of course, they also have the extraordinary

00:19:35.080 --> 00:19:38.580
research opportunity as well.

00:19:38.580 --> 00:19:42.440
Genomics England, as Malcolm has explained,
is the body that is charged with actually

00:19:42.440 --> 00:19:47.710
negotiating contracts with companies capable
of delivering the sequenced results. It is

00:19:47.710 --> 00:19:54.710
engaged in a very complex series of conversations
to that. In that it is certainly supported

00:19:56.129 --> 00:20:03.129
by very significant legal advice. However,
I am not in a position to give specifics.

00:20:03.289 --> 00:20:08.539
We do not have any responsibility in that
connection. Whoever might eventually be selected

00:20:08.539 --> 00:20:13.059
for providing the sequences is a matter for
Genomics England and not for NHS England.

00:20:13.059 --> 00:20:16.749
Therefore, our concern will be to make sure
the programme we run now for selection of

00:20:16.749 --> 00:20:19.999
partners is fair and open. It will be.

00:20:19.999 --> 00:20:26.440
I will also address the point about counselling.
When we say consent, in this context we are

00:20:26.440 --> 00:20:31.429
absolutely talking about counselling. One
of the significant costs of the programmes

00:20:31.429 --> 00:20:35.919
to participating trusts, as they start, will
be the cost of the counselling process. It

00:20:35.919 --> 00:20:42.919
is not just a process in which somebody signs
a form. As Malcolm alluded to, at every level

00:20:44.169 --> 00:20:48.830
there is a very sophisticated ethical and
clinical conversation going on in relation

00:20:48.830 --> 00:20:52.809
to consent. That conversation is about counselling,
the nature of the words, the degree to which

00:20:52.809 --> 00:20:56.769
people can be consenting to something where
we do not quite know the future yet, etc..

00:20:56.769 --> 00:20:59.070
We also, of course, do not know the implications
for themselves and their family.

00:20:59.070 --> 00:21:03.960
This is a hugely complex process which is
being developed as we speak. The areas of

00:21:03.960 --> 00:21:08.120
science in this regard are not just the science
of genetic sequencing. Actually, the science

00:21:08.120 --> 00:21:12.940
of counselling in this is really a new one.
That is all being worked through in great

00:21:12.940 --> 00:21:13.320
detail.

00:21:13.320 --> 00:21:19.029
Malcolm Grant: I would just like to add to
that first part of Tim's answer. The procurement

00:21:19.029 --> 00:21:26.029
of the sequencing is being done through open,
OG compliant, competition. However, it is

00:21:26.230 --> 00:21:31.200
quite a strong wish on the part of Genomics
England that the sequencing is undertaken

00:21:31.200 --> 00:21:37.960
in this country and that samples are not sent
abroad. I think that will also help in the

00:21:37.960 --> 00:21:44.960
continued development of technology in which
scientists in this country have been engaged.

00:21:46.679 --> 00:21:50.889
Bruce, did you also want to respond to part
two of Margaret's question?

00:21:50.889 --> 00:21:56.749
Bruce Keogh: No, I think Tim has done that.
Thank you very much.

00:21:56.749 --> 00:21:58.100
Malcolm Grant: Right.

00:21:58.100 --> 00:21:59.980
Margaret Casely Hayford: Thank you very much.

00:21:59.980 --> 00:22:00.490
Malcolm Grant: Okay. I have Ciaran and Victor.
Ciaran.

00:22:00.490 --> 00:22:02.249
Ciaran Devane: First of all, thank you, Bruce,
for that thing about risk pooling. I had not

00:22:02.249 --> 00:22:07.730
quite clocked that and the implication of
all this. Therefore, thank you for that.

00:22:07.730 --> 00:22:12.029
Slightly glibly, can we make sure that no
one at an NHS institution is using fax machines

00:22:12.029 --> 00:22:16.789
for patient notes? I just could not cope with
the cognitive dissonance of doing this amazing

00:22:16.789 --> 00:22:19.419
technology while at the same time we are doing
some slightly...

00:22:19.419 --> 00:22:22.429
Malcolm Grant: We should strike off any potential
trust that uses fax machines?

00:22:22.429 --> 00:22:29.090
Ciaran Devane: In my humble opinion. It is
a hobby horse.

00:22:29.090 --> 00:22:36.090
However, how do we translate this into health
improvement and health impact? It is the usual

00:22:36.100 --> 00:22:41.970
problem. We are great at inventing things.
However, how is rolling it out on scale to

00:22:41.970 --> 00:22:46.919
really change the lives of people going to
work?

00:22:46.919 --> 00:22:53.919
Bruce Keogh: I think that is part of where
we are going to have to feel our way. In the

00:22:55.710 --> 00:22:59.289
same way, we did not know where some of the
other interventions[?] were going to lead.

00:22:59.289 --> 00:23:06.179
This may be overstating it a bit. However,
imagine if you are a child born in years to

00:23:06.179 --> 00:23:12.600
come and you have your genome sequenced kind
of one day one. There is then a conversation

00:23:12.600 --> 00:23:23.020
with your parents that says your new child
is at risk of cardiovascular disease at one

00:23:23.749 --> 00:23:30.749
level, or may be at risk of colon cancer at
another. Then the child would be brought up

00:23:31.129 --> 00:23:38.960
with preventative measures. There would be,
presumably, dietary interventions and what

00:23:38.960 --> 00:23:43.110
have you. There would be targeted screening
programmes to pick up things at the earliest

00:23:43.110 --> 00:23:49.330
possible stage. That is the sort of direction
of travel that might begin to emerge.

00:23:49.330 --> 00:23:56.330
Tim Kelsey: Can I just add something as well?
Over the last few months and weeks this programme

00:23:56.740 --> 00:23:59.529
has really begun to start mobilising. Even
over that time, what has been really interesting

00:23:59.529 --> 00:24:04.210
is the degree to which, as Malcolm said, the
academic community and enterprise community

00:24:04.210 --> 00:24:10.830
are really beginning to put a load of weight
and investment behind this. Of course, here

00:24:10.830 --> 00:24:16.509
we are in Birmingham University already setting
a standard in thinking differently about bioinformatics

00:24:16.509 --> 00:24:21.009
related industries that will be driven from
this kind of new science.

00:24:21.009 --> 00:24:28.009
I think the opportunity for this country to
be amongst the world's leaders in analytics

00:24:29.080 --> 00:24:35.429
of the kinds of data that will flow from this
is really an important part of how sense will

00:24:35.429 --> 00:24:37.340
be made of it, over time.

00:24:37.340 --> 00:24:38.700
Malcolm Grant: Victor?

00:24:38.700 --> 00:24:45.139
Victor Adebowale: No, I think it is whizz
bang stuff. We have to do it. I was reassured

00:24:45.139 --> 00:24:49.200
by Malcolm's response to Margaret's question.
However, I think there is another question

00:24:49.200 --> 00:24:55.110
which we just need to bear in mind. There
is a question of, almost, how we lead people

00:24:55.110 --> 00:25:00.629
from where we are they have not been yet.
That seems to be the challenge. It strikes

00:25:00.629 --> 00:25:07.629
me that we need to take the public with us.
I just wonder how we do that, in terms with

00:25:09.929 --> 00:25:16.929
everybody engaging with the huge possibilities,
the risks and issues of trust. It is how we

00:25:20.639 --> 00:25:27.639
do that, or indeed who does it, that is important.
Clearly we need to be a world leader in this

00:25:27.830 --> 00:25:32.769
area. However, we also need to be a world
leader in engaging all the public in this.

00:25:32.769 --> 00:25:38.799
It is going to change the policy of health
and social care, not just the practice. That

00:25:38.799 --> 00:25:45.799
is the bit I just want to flag up, really.
It is something we need to either lead, follow

00:25:46.649 --> 00:25:49.850
or even possibly get out of the way of it.
However, we need to be aware of it.

00:25:49.850 --> 00:25:56.850
Malcolm Grant: I think in the early stage
the groups that are being identified amongst

00:26:00.539 --> 00:26:04.360
the list of conditions, particularly rare,
inherited disorders, are enthusiastic about

00:26:04.360 --> 00:26:10.080
participating in the programme. Many of the
cancer groups are also enthusiastic. Therefore,

00:26:10.080 --> 00:26:17.080
in the pilot phase, as it were, there is already
an engaged community. However, your question

00:26:17.139 --> 00:26:17.539
goes beyond that.

00:26:17.539 --> 00:26:18.360
Victor Adebowale: Yes, far beyond that.

00:26:18.360 --> 00:26:23.080
Malcolm Grant: I think it goes into the more
profound implications for the health service.

00:26:23.080 --> 00:26:28.799
I would say, from that, again, it is a pilot.
It is experimental. I think we need to feel

00:26:28.799 --> 00:26:33.700
our way. We need to be able to demonstrate,
actually, for that initial group that there

00:26:33.700 --> 00:26:34.889
are findings that are of value.

00:26:34.889 --> 00:26:40.440
They are not always going to be findings that
are of value to individuals who have had the

00:26:40.440 --> 00:26:46.549
sequencing done of their genome. It is actually
for the populations more generally. However,

00:26:46.549 --> 00:26:53.549
I think the concerns of the Board are well
respected and understood.

00:26:54.799 --> 00:26:59.600
May I then please turn the Board's attention
to the questions that are before you? First

00:26:59.600 --> 00:27:06.600
is the noting of the role that we have, as
NHS England, in supporting the project. I

00:27:07.110 --> 00:27:11.879
think we have rehearsed that fairly well around
the table. However, the second question is

00:27:11.879 --> 00:27:18.879
on the Genomic Medicines Centre selection
process and timetable. Are the Board content

00:27:19.480 --> 00:27:25.460
to endorse that proposal? Right, thank you
very much. Are we content to publish the prospectus

00:27:25.460 --> 00:27:29.320
inviting providers to bid? Fine.

00:27:29.320 --> 00:27:36.070
Well, thank you very much. That is a very
important item of business.

00:27:36.070 --> 00:27:43.070
Normally, in the interests of humankind, after
two hours of a meeting I would suggest a break.

00:27:43.369 --> 00:27:48.679
Can I just check the mood of the meeting.
We have only the reports of the committee

00:27:48.679 --> 00:27:52.369
still to come. Can I check if you would be
willing for me to continue?

00:27:52.369 --> 00:27:54.489
Victor Adebowale: Let us get on with it.

00:27:54.489 --> 00:27:59.749
Malcolm Grant: The body language, if I might
put it like that, seems to be positive.

00:27:59.749 --> 00:28:02.299
Victor Adebowale: Great timing, to ask that
question .

00:28:02.299 --> 00:28:04.679
Malcolm Grant: for us to press ahead.

